      "description": "Learn how the web works, then build and style your own pages with HTML and CSS.",
      "ageBand": "Both",
      "sortOrder": 1,
      "isPublished": true,
      "courses": ["how-the-web-works", "html-basics", "css-basics"]
    },
    {
      "slug": "javascript",
      "title": "JavaScript",
      "description": "Make your pages interactive: variables, decisions, loops, functions, the DOM and events.",
      "ageBand": "Both",
      "sortOrder": 2,
      "isPublished": true,
      "courses": ["javascript-basics"],
      "prerequisites": ["web-foundations"]
    },
    {
      // Right after JavaScript: TypeScript is JavaScript plus types. It's the bridge to C# (static
      // types, interfaces, classes) and comes before Angular, which is written in TypeScript. React
      // doesn't require it, so learners already in the React track aren't blocked.
      "slug": "typescript",
      "title": "TypeScript",
      "description": "Add types to your JavaScript so mistakes show up before your code runs: typed functions, objects, unions and classes.",
      "ageBand": "Both",
      "sortOrder": 3,
      "isPublished": true,
      "courses": ["typescript-basics"],
      "prerequisites": ["javascript"]
    },
    {
      // Angular is written in TypeScript, so the TypeScript track comes first. Same order as the
      // curriculum this platform follows: TypeScript, then Angular, then React.
      "slug": "angular",
      "title": "Angular",
      "description": "Build apps with Google's framework: components, signals, control flow, inputs and outputs, and services.",
      "ageBand": "Both",
      "sortOrder": 4,
      "isPublished": true,
      "courses": ["angular-basics"],
      "prerequisites": ["typescript"]
    },
    {
      // The slug predates the Angular track; only the title changed, so progress and certificates are unaffected.
      "slug": "frontend-frameworks",
      "title": "React",
      "description": "Build apps out of components with React: JSX, props, lists, state, forms and data from a server.",
      "ageBand": "Both",
      "sortOrder": 5,
      "isPublished": true,
      "courses": ["react-basics", "react-apps"],
      "prerequisites": ["javascript"]
    },
    {
      // The first backend track, after the frontend ones (the curriculum's order: React, then C#, then SQL).
      // TypeScript is the prerequisite because it introduces static types, classes and interfaces, which C#
      // builds on. C# runs in the browser on .NET for WebAssembly (src/Codegrove.CSharpRunner.Wasm).
      // Courses: csharp-basics, then csharp-objects (OOP). ASP.NET Core Web API is the web-apis track, after
      // SQL, so its database lessons can build on what the Databases track teaches.
      "slug": "csharp",
      "title": "C#",
      "description": "Write real programs in C#, the language behind ASP.NET Core and Unity: the compiler, variables, decisions, loops, methods and classes, then exceptions, inheritance, interfaces, records and LINQ.",
      "ageBand": "Both",
      "sortOrder": 6,
      "isPublished": true,
      "courses": ["csharp-basics", "csharp-objects"],
      "prerequisites": ["typescript"]
    },
    {
      // After C#, in the curriculum's order. SQL runs in the browser on SQLite (sql.js, see
      // web/src/runner/sqlEngine.ts); lessons use SQL that works the same in SQL Server, and Builder lessons
      // note the differences. Planned courses: sql-basics, then sql-relational (keys, JOINs, design).
      // ASP.NET Core Web API comes after this track, so it can use a real SQL Server database with EF Core.
      "slug": "databases",
      "title": "Databases and SQL",
      "description": "Store data and ask it questions with SQL: tables and SELECT, filtering, sorting, totals, changing data and designing your own tables.",
      "ageBand": "Both",
      "sortOrder": 7,
      "isPublished": true,
      "courses": ["sql-basics"],
      "prerequisites": ["csharp"]
    },
    {
      // C# course 3: ASP.NET Core Web API, after both C# and SQL (the curriculum's order), leading into the
      // capstone. Lessons 1-5 (HTTP, routes, request bodies, services, controllers) need only C#; lessons 6-7
      // use Entity Framework Core and build on the Databases track. Servers run on Codegrove's practice
      // server in the browser (src/Codegrove.WebShim), with the real ASP.NET Core names.
      "slug": "web-apis",
      "title": "Web APIs with C#",
      "description": "Build the server side of an app with ASP.NET Core: answer HTTP requests, read and validate what clients send, organize code with services and controllers, and save data with Entity Framework Core.",
      "ageBand": "Both",
      "sortOrder": 8,
      "isPublished": true,
      "courses": ["csharp-web-api"],
      "prerequisites": ["csharp", "databases"]
    }
  ],
